# AGENTS.md

## Project context

This repository is a fork of `zed-industries/zed`.

* Fork: `starikovyaroslav/zed`
* Upstream: `zed-industries/zed`
* Main project epic: GitHub issue #1
* Work must be performed through the child issues linked from issue #1.

The goal is to build a native, editor-integrated advanced Git workflow for Zed comparable in completeness to JetBrains/WebStorm while preserving Zed's architecture, UX conventions and performance characteristics.

## Language

All reports intended for the repository owner must be written in Russian.

This includes:

* task progress summaries;
* final implementation reports;
* explanations of architectural decisions;
* descriptions of blockers;
* test results communicated to the owner.

Code, identifiers, commit messages, branch names, pull request titles and technical GitHub issue comments should remain in English unless Russian is explicitly required.

## Issue workflow

Work on only one implementation issue at a time unless the issue explicitly requires a coordinated change.

Before starting an issue:

1. Read the parent epic #1.
2. Read the selected child issue completely.
3. Read all issue comments.
4. Inspect related upstream issues, pull requests and current code.
5. Verify that the requested functionality has not already been implemented.
6. Review the dependencies listed in the issue.
7. Inspect `main`, the current working tree and existing open pull requests.
8. Post a short English comment in the issue stating that work has started and naming the planned branch.

Do not mark an issue completed merely because a prototype exists.

## Branches

Create a dedicated branch for every issue.

Use:

```text
feat/issue-<number>-<short-description>
fix/issue-<number>-<short-description>
refactor/issue-<number>-<short-description>
```

Examples:

```text
feat/issue-2-git-operation-state
feat/issue-4-multi-cherry-pick
feat/issue-8-interactive-rebase
```

Always branch from the latest appropriate base branch.

Before creating a branch:

```bash
git fetch origin
git fetch upstream
git status
```

Do not commit unrelated local changes.

## Commits

Create meaningful, focused commits.

Commit messages must be in English and use an issue reference:

```text
feat(git): add recoverable operation state (#2)
test(git): cover interrupted cherry-pick recovery (#2)
docs(git): document operation recovery workflow (#2)
```

Do not create one giant final commit when the implementation naturally consists of multiple coherent changes.

Do not create commits that contain:

* temporary debugging output;
* generated build artifacts;
* unrelated formatting;
* secrets;
* credentials;
* local environment files.

## Implementation requirements

Before editing code:

1. Investigate the current repository architecture.
2. Identify actual crates, modules, types and conventions.
3. Search for related work in the current branch and upstream repository.
4. Create a concrete implementation plan.
5. Then proceed directly to implementation.

Do not stop after writing a plan.

Do not:

* invent APIs without checking the repository;
* create a parallel Git subsystem;
* place raw Git command construction inside UI components;
* implement only a visual mock;
* leave critical functionality as TODO;
* declare completion while tests fail;
* silently remove or weaken existing behavior;
* bypass existing Zed abstractions without a documented reason.

Related architectural refactoring is allowed when necessary for a complete and maintainable solution.

## GitHub issue updates

Use `gh` to communicate progress.

At the start, add a short comment containing:

* branch name;
* implementation scope;
* important dependencies or discovered overlap.

During implementation, comment only when there is a meaningful event:

* architecture materially differs from the original issue;
* an upstream implementation overlaps with the task;
* an external blocker is discovered;
* the issue scope must be split;
* a partial PR is intentionally created.

Do not spam the issue with low-level progress updates.

At completion, add a detailed English issue comment containing:

* what was implemented;
* architecture summary;
* key files and components;
* tests executed;
* test results;
* known limitations;
* pull request link.

The private/user-facing summary in Codex must contain the same information in Russian.

## Pull requests

For each completed issue:

1. Ensure the branch is pushed to `origin`.
2. Open a pull request against `starikovyaroslav/zed`.
3. Use a clear English title.
4. Include `Closes #<issue>` only when the issue Definition of Done is completely satisfied.
5. Otherwise use `Related to #<issue>`.
6. Include implementation summary, architecture, tests, screenshots where relevant and known limitations.
7. Keep the PR in draft state until the implementation is ready for review.

Never open a pull request against `zed-industries/zed` without explicit owner approval.

Do not merge pull requests automatically unless explicitly instructed.

## Verification

Run the most relevant checks supported by the repository.

At minimum:

* formatting for affected Rust code;
* targeted tests;
* compilation or checks for affected crates;
* applicable UI tests;
* existing Git-related regression tests.

If a complete test suite is impractical, run focused checks and explicitly state what was not run and why.

Do not claim that a test passed unless it was actually executed successfully.

## Destructive operations

Do not:

* force-push shared branches;
* rewrite `main`;
* delete branches;
* close issues;
* merge pull requests;
* modify upstream remotes;
* discard user changes;

unless the owner explicitly requested that exact action.

Force-push is allowed only on the agent's dedicated issue branch when necessary after a rebase and must use `--force-with-lease`.

## Completion criteria

An issue is complete only when:

* its Definition of Done is satisfied;
* implementation is not a mock or prototype;
* relevant tests pass;
* code is committed;
* branch is pushed;
* draft or ready PR is created;
* issue contains a completion comment;
* final Russian report is provided to the owner.

If any requirement remains incomplete, clearly say that the issue is only partially implemented.

